    "media:imagetext": "October 2nd, we made a short tour of Hamburg in the P.A.A. \"Volkswagen\" and\ntook a Sabena (Belgian Airline) to Dusseldorf, Brussels and London. We stayed\nat Claridges in London Thursday and Friday nights and Saturday morning took\nAir Chance to Paris. There I left the Madam safely ensconced in the Roblin\nand caught a P.A.A. scheduled 8 P.M. departure for New York.\nLater - enroute Gander-Boston. I have had an uneventful\ncrossing. Paris to Shannon, then Gander, Newfoundland and now we're over\nNova Scotia sparkling in brillant sunshine 10,000 feet below. On board is a\nvery interesting man who represents the wool growers of 13 Western States. The\nU. S. Wool industry has been on the decline because of the scarcity of sheep\nherders. It seems that our boys would rather take their girls to the movies\nor hang around the corner drug store than wander off alone in the mountsins for\nmonths at a time doing a pied piper routine with a lot of sheep. As a result\nthe wool growers association got some legislation through Congress to permit\nthe immigration of Basque sheep herders. Some 3000 will be flown all the way\nfrom the Pyrenees to the Rockies. 500 will be flown this year starting tomorrow\nand 2500 next year. All of them will fly with P.A.A. to New York and I.W.A.\nacross the U.S.A. Many of these boys have never ridden in an automobile. Most\nof them speak only Bascue. We pin a big badge on each one which he is told is\nhis ticket. It tells where he is going and how. Also if he strays from the fold\nthe finder is asked in French and English to lead him Jo a P.A.A. office. In the\nTest they receive $200. per month plus their board, lodgings, if any, tobacco and\na California wine, equivalent of Spanish wine. One Ranch owner told my friend\nthat these herders let their salaries accumulate for so many years that the\nRancher finally settles the account by taking them into partnership rather than\ndeplete his working capital by paying their back wages. He said that something\nlike 80% of our Western sheep herders are Basques. Its a nice piece of business\nfor P.A.A. because most of them will be carried when westbound loads are light\nand the gross will be net.",
